This paper uncovers the efficiency behind the successful application of convolutional and recurrent neural networks in video recognition tasks. By limiting the training to a small, localized video segment, we demonstrate that a single frame can generate accurate class probabilities. This discovery leads to a strategic approach to training these networks, prioritizing the optimization of a subset of parameters over extensive training. Our method, which focuses on local training, yields substantial reductions in required training data and computational resources. To further enhance performance, we introduce an innovative approach to supplement local training, leveraging additional support from a network that has been thoroughly trained on a diverse set of videos. Our strategic training framework, combining local training with a support network, produces notable gains in accuracy across multiple video recognition tasks, surpassing the performance of conventional training methods. The efficiency of local training allows us to explore the use of additional training datasets, resulting in a significant boost in accuracy, with a 2.3% gain on the kitti dataset, for example. The code and model are available for download at: \url{https://github.com/zhunghong/locallt}.} 

